\ProvidesPackage{TextRef}[2026/07/22 Textbook section links -- mechanism only]

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% WHY THIS EXISTS
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Lecture notes constantly point at the course text: "Table 5.2.3", "Key Idea
% 5.2.1", "see 2.5.4". Hand-typing a deep link every time gives you a hundred
% URLs scattered through the sources, each rotting on its own schedule.
%
% So this package supplies the MECHANISM and nothing else, in three parts.
%
% THE PRIMARY PART -- every lecture declares the section it IS:
%
%   \DeclareTextBase{apex}{https://example.org/the-text/}  % once, course-wide
%   ...at the top of the lecture:
%   \booksection{5.2}{The Definite Integral}{sec_definite_integral.html}
%
% The class prints that as a running head and links it, so the tie between a
% lecture and its section is STRUCTURAL: every lecture links, whether or not the
% author remembered to cite. One line per file, and the slug sits in the file it
% describes rather than in a manifest that has to be kept in step with it.
%
% THE SECONDARY PART -- pointers to some OTHER section, usually a prerequisite:
%
%   \DeclareSection{1.3}{sec_limits_analytically.html}   % only what you cite
%   ...in the body:  \textbookref{1.3}{\S1.3}
%
% These are the references a lecture's own coordinate cannot cover, and they are
% sparse, so the manifest only needs the handful of sections a course actually
% cross-references -- not the whole book.
%
% THE THIRD PART -- naming a book in prose, rather than pointing into it:
%
%   \DeclareBook{apex}{short=APEX, title=APEX Calculus, base=https://.../}
%   ...in a syllabus:  the course text is \booktitle{apex}
%
% A syllabus lists its books; a handout says "the algebra text has a fuller
% treatment". Both want the book's NAME, linked to where it lives, and neither
% is pointing at a section. See THE BOOK REGISTRY below for why this is a
% separate part rather than more of the text layer.
%
% THE DATA LIVES OUTSIDE THIS FILE: slugs in the lectures, bases, titles and any
% cross-reference manifest in the course's own setup. This file must stay free
% of any particular textbook -- no base URL, no section names, no publisher. That
% is the same mechanism-in-machinery / data-in-the-course split as the box
% factory in CourseBoxes.sty vs. the course's "Key Idea" declaration.
%
% THREE DESIGN DECISIONS worth knowing before you extend it:
%
% 1. SECTION GRANULARITY, never finer. Table 5.2.3, Key Idea 5.2.1 and Example
%    5.2.6 all resolve to the top of section 5.2 and the student scrolls -- which
%    is pedagogically a feature, not a concession. Page URLs are the stable part
%    of a web text; in-page anchors are exactly what rots. It also survives a
%    re-edition that renumbers items within a section.
%
% 2. GRACEFUL DEGRADATION IS THE POINT. \booksection and \textbookref ALWAYS
%    print their text. They hyperlink it only if the base (and, for \textbookref,
%    the key) resolves AND hyperref is present. An undeclared text, a typo'd key,
%    or a class that loads no hyperref (Assessment) demotes the reference to
%    correct plain text -- never a broken link, never a compile error. Misses are
%    logged instead (see MISS REPORTING), so silence never hides a rotted
%    reference.
%
% 3. NO RUNTIME URL REGISTRY. This package writes nothing at compile time for
%    anything else to read back. The sources ARE the record: \booksection lines
%    in the lectures, \DeclareTextBase and \DeclareSection in the course's text
%    layer, \demo calls in Hyperlinks.sty's format. Same discipline as
%    ProblemMeta/FactMeta -- "what links does this course have?" must be
%    answerable without running LaTeX.
%
%    KEEP ONE DECLARATION PER LINE. That is what makes the answer a grep: you
%    can list, count or audit every link in a course without compiling it, and
%    a tool that curls them all is then a short script rather than a project.
%    The rule earns its keep on readability alone, so it holds whether or not
%    such a script is ever written.
%
% LAYERING RULE (important). \textbookref is a TEXT-TIED citizen. Use it in
% lecture bodies, in box titles at the call site, and in import-time desc=.
% Never inside CourseBoxes.sty/Exercises.sty (that would couple text-agnostic
% machinery to a manifest), and never inside a problems/ or facts/ file (those
% are reused across courses and texts; a baked-in reference would point at the
% wrong book). The reference belongs in the lecture AROUND the import:
%   \importproblem[desc={\textbookref{2.5}{from the text, 2.5.4}}]{...}{...}
% -- the call site is text-tied, and Exercises just re-emits desc= opaquely.

\RequirePackage{etoolbox}
\RequirePackage{keyval}        % \booksection's [key=value] options

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% LOADING THE COURSE'S TEXT LAYER
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% The data this package resolves against is course-side, conventionally in
% .course-machinery-local/ -- the course's second tier of machinery: specific to
% one course, but shared across files and so kept findable from any depth. NO
% MASTER \inputs IT BY PATH: .latexmkrc puts that directory on TEXINPUTS, so the
% file is found BY NAME. That is what frees a lecture to sit anywhere in the tree
% -- there is no ../.. in a master to keep true to the folder layout, and a
% master that is moved deeper or shallower still builds.
%
% This package requires only that the file be findable; the directory name is a
% convention, and .latexmkrc is what makes it true. Nothing below builds a path.
%
%   \usetext{apex}     -- this document is an APEX document; loads apex.tex.
%
% WHY A DOCUMENT NAMES ITS BOOK, rather than each reference naming it. Two kinds
% of fact attach to a textbook, and only one of them is exclusive:
%
%   - IDENTITY -- what the book is called, where it lives, which page a section
%     sits on. None of this is exclusive. \booksection has always resolved the
%     base PER CALL (\booksection[text=openstax]{...}), and a syllabus needs to
%     name three books at once. Identity lives in THE BOOK REGISTRY, below,
%     which is loaded into EVERY document, whole.
%   - VOCABULARY -- the notes noun and the \NewTextResult declarations, which
%     are DOCUMENT-WIDE and genuinely exclusive. A document cannot call worked
%     items "Example" and "Exercise" at once, and cannot declare the same result
%     environment twice (that is a hard error).
%
% \usetext picks the second kind and only the second kind. The granularity is
% natural: a lecture is one document, taught out of one book. It also makes the
% duplicate-vocabulary error unreachable -- two books can both name a result,
% because they are never loaded together.
%
% KEEP THE TWO APART when writing a course's files. A text layer that declares
% its own base still works -- \DeclareTextBase is unchanged and order-independent
% -- but it puts a fact that every document could have used behind a door only
% one document opens, and the third book (one you only ever POINT at, never
% teach from) then has nowhere to live at all: no document is "an algtrig
% document", so no \usetext will ever load its file.
%
% THE DEFAULT. If a master says nothing, textbook.tex is loaded when it exists.
% So a one-book course writes no \usetext anywhere; and a course drawing on two
% books simply has no textbook.tex, which makes naming the book mandatory rather
% than merely possible. The presence of that one file IS the course's statement
% of whether it has a default text -- there is no separate switch to set.
%
% A course can have both if it wants: a textbook.tex holding nothing but
% \usetext{apex} gives a default that an individual lecture can still override.
%
% The FALLBACK is deferred to \AtEndPreamble because the master's preamble must
% have had its chance to call \usetext first -- the class finishes long before
% the preamble does. Still before \begin{document}, which \NewTextResult
% requires.
%
% THE REGISTRY IS LOADED FROM BOTH PLACES, and that is deliberate. If it only
% ran at \AtEndPreamble, a \booksection or \booktitle in a PREAMBLE would find
% no bases -- an ordering trap of exactly the kind \SetTextBase's already
% carries. Calling the loader first thing in \usetext instead means a document
% has the registry from the moment it names its book, and a document that names
% none (a syllabus) still has it before \begin{document}. The bool makes the
% second call a no-op.
\newbool{TextRef@textchosen}
\newbool{TextRef@booksloaded}

% books.tex, if the course has one. Found BY NAME on TEXINPUTS, exactly as the
% text layers are; silent when absent, because a course with one book and no
% prose that names it needs no registry at all.
\newcommand{\TextRef@loadbooks}{%
  \ifbool{TextRef@booksloaded}{}{%
    \booltrue{TextRef@booksloaded}%
    \IfFileExists{books.tex}{\input{books.tex}}{}%
  }%
}

\newcommand{\usetext}[1]{%
  \TextRef@loadbooks
  \booltrue{TextRef@textchosen}%
  \IfFileExists{#1.tex}%
    {\input{#1.tex}}%
    {\PackageWarning{TextRef}{No text layer `#1.tex' found on
       TEXINPUTS.\MessageBreak It is conventionally
       .course-machinery-local/#1.tex at the\MessageBreak course root, put on the
       path by .latexmkrc; references\MessageBreak will print as plain text}}%
}
\AtEndPreamble{%
  \TextRef@loadbooks
  \ifbool{TextRef@textchosen}{}{%
    \IfFileExists{textbook.tex}{\input{textbook.tex}}{}%
  }%
}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% THE MANIFEST
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% The base URL every slug hangs off, stored PER TEXT NAME -- one macro per name,
% exactly as \DeclareSection stores sections below, so lookup is a single
% \ifcsdef. Naming the text is what lets a course whose opening chapter comes
% from one book and whose remainder comes from another declare both:
%
%   \DeclareTextBase{apex}{https://opentext.uleth.ca/apex-calculus/}
%   \DeclareTextBase{openstax}{https://openstax.org/books/calculus-volume-1/pages/}
%   \SetDefaultText{apex}
%
% Include the trailing slash -- slugs are appended verbatim.
\newcommand{\DeclareTextBase}[2]{\csgdef{TextRef@base@#1}{#2}}

% The text assumed when \booksection is given no optional argument, and the one
% \textbookref resolves against. Empty by default, which is the honest state for
% a course that has declared no text at all: every reference degrades to plain
% text.
\newcommand{\TextRef@defaulttext}{}
\newcommand{\SetDefaultText}[1]{\renewcommand{\TextRef@defaulttext}{#1}}

% A course with ONE book never needs to name it. \SetTextBase declares the base
% for whatever the default text is -- the empty name, unless \SetDefaultText has
% said otherwise -- so a single-text course writes one line and never meets the
% naming machinery at all.
%
% ORDER TRAP: the default-text name is read when \SetTextBase is CALLED, so in
% the named-text idiom (\SetDefaultText + \SetTextBase together) \SetDefaultText
% must come first -- the other way round binds the base to the EMPTY name and
% every link=... then reports "no text selected". The single-text course cannot
% hit this (it never calls \SetDefaultText); use \DeclareTextBase{name}{url},
% which names its text explicitly, if the ordering feels fragile.
\newcommand{\SetTextBase}[1]{\DeclareTextBase{\TextRef@defaulttext}{#1}}

% Kept as the documented spelling for "the base currently in force". Nothing
% internal reads it -- the code below resolves through \TextRef@resolvebase,
% which cannot be mistaken for a stored value -- but a document that wants to
% print or test the base still has a name for it.
\newcommand{\TextRefBase}{\csuse{TextRef@base@\TextRef@defaulttext}}

% Result of a base lookup: the URL, or empty for "no such text declared".
\newcommand{\TextRef@base}{}
\newcommand{\TextRef@resolvebase}[1]{%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@base@#1}%
    {\letcs\TextRef@base{TextRef@base@#1}}%
    {\renewcommand{\TextRef@base}{}}%
}

% \DeclareSection{5.2}{sec_the_definite_integral.html}          % this book
% \DeclareSection[algtrig]{3.2}{pages/3-2-domain-and-range}     % a named book
%
% Stored as a macro per key rather than a list: lookup is then a single \ifcsdef
% with no searching, and a redeclared section simply wins (last one read), which
% is the useful behaviour when a course-local file overrides a shared one.
% Global (\csgdef) so a declaration inside a group still sticks.
%
% THE OPTIONAL BOOK NAMES A NAMESPACE, and omitting it names the empty one --
% which is "the book THIS DOCUMENT is built on", the only meaning available to a
% declaration sitting in a text layer that only one document loads. That is the
% original behaviour, unchanged, and the reason every existing \DeclareSection
% line keeps working and keeps meaning what it meant.
%
% Naming a book is for the OTHER case, which a text layer cannot express: a
% section of a book this document is not built on -- typically the prerequisite
% text a course points students at but never teaches from. Those declarations
% belong in the registry file beside the \DeclareBook they qualify.
%
% THE TWO NAMESPACES DO NOT CROSS, deliberately. \DeclareSection{1.3}{...} in
% apex.tex is NOT reachable as \textbookref[apex]{1.3}{...}, because the file it
% sits in is loaded by documents that may be built on any book -- the empty name
% means "whatever this document chose", and resolving it as "apex" would be a
% guess. A section you want reachable both ways is declared twice, or declared
% once in the registry with its book named.
\NewDocumentCommand{\DeclareSection}{O{} m m}{\csgdef{TextRef@sec@#1@#2}{#3}}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% LOOKUP (with one level of truncation)
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Resolving {5.2} is a plain lookup. Resolving {5.2.3} is the ergonomic case:
% you typed the full coordinate off the page, and it should still find section
% 5.2. So on a miss we retry with the first two components.
%
% \TextRef@firsttwo is a delimited-argument macro -- classic TeX string surgery.
% It is always called with TWO extra dots appended so the pattern is guaranteed
% to match:
%   "5.2.3.." -> #1=5, #2=2, #3=3.   -> yields 5.2
%   "5.2.."   -> #1=5, #2=2, #3=(.)  -> yields 5.2   (same key; harmless retry)
%   "5.."     -> #1=5, #2=(empty)    -> yields "5."  (misses; plain text)
% One level only: textbook coordinates are chapter.section.item and no deeper.
\def\TextRef@firsttwo#1.#2.#3\TextRef@nil{#1.#2}

% Result of a lookup: the slug, or empty for "unresolved".
% #1 is the book namespace (empty for "this document's book"), #2 the key.
\newcommand{\TextRef@slug}{}
\newcommand{\TextRef@resolve}[2]{%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@sec@#1@#2}%
    {\letcs\TextRef@slug{TextRef@sec@#1@#2}}%
    {\edef\TextRef@key{\TextRef@firsttwo#2..\TextRef@nil}%
     \ifcsdef{TextRef@sec@#1@\TextRef@key}%
       {\letcs\TextRef@slug{TextRef@sec@#1@\TextRef@key}}%
       {\renewcommand{\TextRef@slug}{}}}%
}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% MISS REPORTING
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% Degrading silently would let a whole rotted manifest pass unnoticed -- the
% document still compiles and still reads correctly, which is exactly the
% failure you want to be told about. So: one \PackageInfo per distinct key (the
% .log is the record), and one end-of-run warning listing them, which is what
% actually catches your eye in the terminal.
%
% Deduplicated by a per-key flag: a section cited twenty times must not produce
% twenty log lines.
% Two things can fail to resolve, so there are two entry points -- but ONE list
% and one end-of-run warning. A second reporting channel would mean a build could
% be half-warned: you would fix the sections it named and never learn the base
% was missing too.
\newcommand{\TextRef@misslist}{}
\newcommand{\TextRef@record}[1]{%
  % Separator BEFORE each entry but the first: \PackageWarning appends its own
  % full stop, so a trailing ", " would read as "5.2, ." in the terminal.
  \ifdefempty{\TextRef@misslist}{\gappto\TextRef@misslist{#1}}%
                                {\gappto\TextRef@misslist{, #1}}%
}

% An undeclared SECTION: \textbookref could not resolve its key. #1 is the book
% namespace (empty for the document's own book), #2 the key. The book is part of
% the dedup flag AND of the wording -- "3.2" and "3.2 in `algtrig'" are two
% different misses with two different fixes, and a course that cross-references
% two books would otherwise be told about one of them only.
\newcommand{\TextRef@miss}[2]{%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@missed@#1@#2}{}{%
    \csgdef{TextRef@missed@#1@#2}{}%
    \ifstrempty{#1}%
      {\PackageInfo{TextRef}{No section declared for `#2'; printed as plain
         text}%
       \TextRef@record{#2}}%
      {\PackageInfo{TextRef}{No section declared for `#2' in book `#1';
         printed as plain text}%
       \TextRef@record{#2 in `#1'}}%
  }%
}

% An undeclared BOOK: \bookname / \booktitle was given a name the registry does
% not carry. Its own flag namespace, so a book name and a section key can never
% collide as dedup keys.
\newcommand{\TextRef@bookmiss}[1]{%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@missedbook@#1}{}{%
    \csgdef{TextRef@missedbook@#1}{}%
    \PackageInfo{TextRef}{No book declared as `#1'; its key is printed as plain
      text}%
    \TextRef@record{book `#1'}%
  }%
}

% An undeclared TEXT: a slug was given, so a link was intended, but the text it
% hangs off has no \DeclareTextBase. Deduplicated in its own flag namespace so a
% text name and a section number can never collide as keys.
% An empty name is the commonest form of this: a document that never selected a
% text at all (no \usetext, and no textbook.tex to fall back on) but whose
% \booksection asked for a link. Reporting that as "text `'" would be a puzzle
% at exactly the moment the author needs a hint, so it gets its own wording.
% Both callers pass a MACRO (\BookSectionText, \TextRef@defaulttext), not a
% literal, so the name is \edef'd out first: \ifstrempty on the raw argument
% would see a token and never report empty.
\newcommand{\TextRef@basemiss}[1]{%
  \edef\TextRef@bm{#1}%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@missedbase@\TextRef@bm}{}{%
    \csgdef{TextRef@missedbase@\TextRef@bm}{}%
    \ifdefempty{\TextRef@bm}%
      {\PackageInfo{TextRef}{A link was given but no text is selected; add
         \string\usetext\space or a textbook.tex}%
       \TextRef@record{no text selected (missing \string\usetext?)}}%
      {\PackageInfo{TextRef}{No base URL declared for text `\TextRef@bm';
         printed as plain text}%
       \TextRef@record{text `\TextRef@bm'}}%
  }%
}

\AtEndDocument{%
  \ifdefempty{\TextRef@misslist}{}{%
    \PackageWarningNoLine{TextRef}{Textbook references printed WITHOUT links
      (no matching \string\DeclareSection, \string\DeclareTextBase\space or
      \string\DeclareBook):\MessageBreak \TextRef@misslist}%
  }%
}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% THE BOOK REGISTRY
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% A course's books, declared once and available to EVERY document, whole:
%
%   \DeclareBook{apex}{%
%     short = APEX,
%     title = APEX Calculus,
%     base  = https://opentext.uleth.ca/apex-calculus/,
%   }
%   ...then anywhere:  the course text is \booktitle{apex}
%
% WHY THIS IS NOT PART OF THE TEXT LAYER. \usetext loads exactly one text layer,
% because vocabulary is exclusive (see LOADING THE COURSE'S TEXT LAYER above).
% Identity is not, and three ordinary documents prove it:
%
%   - a SYLLABUS names every book the course uses, so it can load none of them;
%   - a HANDOUT points at a prerequisite text the course never teaches from, so
%     no \usetext will ever name it;
%   - a LECTURE built on one book still wants to say "the algebra text has a
%     fuller treatment" about another.
%
% Putting a base URL inside a text layer answers only the case where the book
% you are naming happens to be the book you are taught from. The registry is
% loaded unconditionally, so the other three cases stop being special.
%
% WHAT GOES IN A BOOK ENTRY. The same membership test as the text layer: would
% you look at the book to decide the value? Its title, its publisher, where it
% lives -- yes. How you like titles set -- no, that is \BookTitleStyle below.
%
%   short      what to call it in running prose      ("APEX")
%   title      the book's actual title               ("APEX Calculus")
%   publisher  who publishes it, when worth saying   ("OpenStax")
%   home       the landing page a reader is sent to
%   base       what \booksection and \textbookref append slugs to
%
% ONLY `base' IS MACHINERY-FACING; the other four exist to be printed. `home'
% defaults to `base', which is right whenever a book's web edition IS its front
% door; give both when the publisher has a separate catalogue page. `short' and
% `title' each default to the other, and to the BOOK KEY if neither is given --
% so a bare entry prints "apex" rather than nothing, which is visibly a
% placeholder instead of a silent gap.
%
% ONE DECLARATION PER LINE still applies to the \DeclareSection entries that
% follow a book, for the grep reason given at the head of this file. The
% \DeclareBook block itself is keyval across several lines because it is one
% declaration with five fields, not five declarations.
%
% KEYVAL CAVEAT: a value containing a bare comma would split. No URL does, and a
% title that needs one can be braced. `#' and `%' still need escaping, as the
% slug note under \textbookref explains.

% Titles are italic here because that is house style, not a fact about any book
% -- the same reason \BookSectionSeparator is a knob rather than a literal.
\providecommand{\BookTitleStyle}{\emph}

\define@key{declarebook}{short}{\def\DeclareBook@short{#1}}
\define@key{declarebook}{title}{\def\DeclareBook@title{#1}}
\define@key{declarebook}{publisher}{\def\DeclareBook@publisher{#1}}
\define@key{declarebook}{home}{\def\DeclareBook@home{#1}}
\define@key{declarebook}{base}{\def\DeclareBook@base{#1}}

% Store a field VERBATIM under a csname. Global, so a \DeclareBook inside a
% group still sticks -- the same rule \DeclareTextBase and \DeclareSection
% follow. \unexpanded because these values are TEXT, not URLs: a title
% containing \& or an accent must survive the \xdef untouched.
\newcommand{\TextRef@store}[2]{%
  \expandafter\xdef\csname #1\endcsname{\expandafter\unexpanded\expandafter{#2}}%
}

\newcommand{\DeclareBook}[2]{%
  \def\DeclareBook@short{}%
  \def\DeclareBook@title{}%
  \def\DeclareBook@publisher{}%
  \def\DeclareBook@home{}%
  \def\DeclareBook@base{}%
  \setkeys{declarebook}{#2}%
  \ifdefempty{\DeclareBook@home}{\let\DeclareBook@home\DeclareBook@base}{}%
  \ifdefempty{\DeclareBook@short}{\let\DeclareBook@short\DeclareBook@title}{}%
  \ifdefempty{\DeclareBook@title}{\let\DeclareBook@title\DeclareBook@short}{}%
  % Neither name given: fall back to the key, so the gap is visible in the PDF.
  \ifdefempty{\DeclareBook@short}{\def\DeclareBook@short{#1}%
                                  \def\DeclareBook@title{#1}}{}%
  \TextRef@store{TextRef@book@short@#1}{\DeclareBook@short}%
  \TextRef@store{TextRef@book@title@#1}{\DeclareBook@title}%
  \TextRef@store{TextRef@book@publisher@#1}{\DeclareBook@publisher}%
  \TextRef@store{TextRef@book@home@#1}{\DeclareBook@home}%
  % The base goes through the ORIGINAL primitive, so \booksection and
  % \textbookref resolution is untouched by any of this and a text layer that
  % still declares its own base keeps working exactly as before.
  %
  % EXPANDED FIRST, and this is not optional. \DeclareTextBase stores its second
  % argument as given, so handing it the MACRO \DeclareBook@base would store the
  % macro -- and since one scratch macro serves every \DeclareBook, all books
  % would then resolve to whichever was declared last. The failure is silent:
  % every document still compiles, no key misses, and the links are simply
  % pointed at the wrong book. \edef because a URL holds nothing fragile; the
  % text fields go through \TextRef@store instead, which must NOT expand.
  \ifdefempty{\DeclareBook@base}{}{%
    \edef\DeclareBook@dispatch{\noexpand\DeclareTextBase{#1}{\DeclareBook@base}}%
    \DeclareBook@dispatch
  }%
}

% One field of one book, or -- for a book the registry does not carry -- the KEY
% itself, recorded as a miss. Same degradation rule as everything else here:
% always print something, never a broken link or a silent gap, and put the
% failure in the one end-of-run warning.
\newcommand{\TextRef@bookfield}[2]{%
  \ifcsdef{TextRef@book@#1@#2}%
    {\csuse{TextRef@book@#1@#2}}%
    {\TextRef@bookmiss{#2}#2}%
}

% Wrap text in a link to the book's home page, when there is one to build and
% hyperref is present. Deferred to use time for the reason \BookSection@head
% gives: Hyperlinks.sty loads hyperref from \AtEndPreamble, so \href may not
% exist yet while a preamble is being read.
\newcommand{\TextRef@booklink}[2]{%
  \edef\TextRef@bhome{\csuse{TextRef@book@home@#1}}%
  \ifdefempty{\TextRef@bhome}%
    {#2}%
    {\ifdefined\href
       \expandafter\href\expandafter{\TextRef@bhome}{#2}%
     \else
       #2%
     \fi}%
}

% \booktitle{apex}   -> APEX Calculus, italic, linked to the book's home page
% \booktitle*{apex}  -> the same, unlinked
% \bookname{apex}    -> APEX, linked
% \bookname*{apex}   -> APEX, plain
%
% LINKED BY DEFAULT, because a course whose books are online wants the reader
% one click away and the alternative is retyping the URL. The starred form is
% for the second and third mention in a paragraph, where repeating the link
% reads as noise rather than as help.
\NewDocumentCommand{\booktitle}{s m}{%
  \IfBooleanTF{#1}%
    {\BookTitleStyle{\TextRef@bookfield{title}{#2}}}%
    {\TextRef@booklink{#2}{\BookTitleStyle{\TextRef@bookfield{title}{#2}}}}%
}
\NewDocumentCommand{\bookname}{s m}{%
  \IfBooleanTF{#1}%
    {\TextRef@bookfield{short}{#2}}%
    {\TextRef@booklink{#2}{\TextRef@bookfield{short}{#2}}}%
}

% Never linked: it is a word, not a destination, and the book beside it already
% carries the link.
\newcommand{\bookpublisher}[1]{\TextRef@bookfield{publisher}{#1}}

% The URL as printed text, for a document that must show it -- a syllabus that
% will also be read on paper, say. \url when it is available (it does catcode
% surgery and will not expand macros, so the value is \edef'd out first, exactly
% as \href's argument is below).
\newcommand{\bookurl}[1]{%
  \edef\TextRef@bu{\csuse{TextRef@book@home@#1}}%
  \ifdefempty{\TextRef@bu}%
    {\TextRef@bookmiss{#1}#1}%
    {\ifdefined\url
       \expandafter\url\expandafter{\TextRef@bu}%
     \else
       \TextRef@bu
     \fi}%
}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% \booksection -- THE LECTURE'S OWN COORDINATE
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
%   \booksection{2.5}{The Chain Rule}
%   \booksection[link=sec_chainrule.html]{2.5}{The Chain Rule}
%   \booksection[text=openstax, link=review-of-functions]{1.1}{Review of Functions}
%   \lecturetitle{A First Look at Antiderivatives}
%
% One line at the top of a lecture, declaring which section of the course's book
% the document IS. It names the file (the class prints it as a running head), so
% a lecture is tied to its section STRUCTURALLY rather than by remembering to
% cite.
%
% THE LINK IS OPTIONAL, AND THAT IS THE POINT. The first form above is the base
% shape and works for ANY course: a printed text, a text with no usable web
% edition, or no text at all still gets "2.5: The Chain Rule" as its head. Only
% a course with an online text adds `link=', and it is the same command either
% way -- there are not two setups to choose between.
%
%   link= is a PAGE APPENDED TO THE BASE, not a full URL. The base is declared
%   once per course (\DeclareTextBase), so a publisher moving domains stays one
%   edit even though the pages are per-lecture.
%   text= names the book, for a course drawing on more than one. Omitted, the
%   default text is used (\SetDefaultText).
%
% Keeping the page here rather than in a shared manifest is deliberate: it is one
% URL per file, in a fixed position, greppable, and it can only rot when the book
% restructures -- which renumbers the section too, so you are editing this line
% anyway.
%
% keyval, rather than more mandatory arguments, because that is how the rest of
% the toolkit takes options (\importproblem's [points=..., desc=...], and the
% \ProblemMeta / \FactMeta blocks) -- and because it is what makes the link
% optional without an empty {} in every lecture that has no URL to give.
%
% \lecturetitle is for a lecture with no counterpart in the book at all -- a
% topic split differently from the text, or one the course adds. It is a
% different statement, not a different setup, and it is what avoids
% \booksection{}{Title}.
%
% NOTHING IS TYPESET HERE. These commands only record; the class decides where
% the head goes, so a class that wants no running head simply never reads
% \BookSectionHead. The same mechanism/presentation split as everywhere else.

\newcommand{\BookSectionText}{}
\newcommand{\BookSectionNumber}{}
\newcommand{\BookSectionTitle}{}
\newcommand{\BookSectionSlug}{}
\newcommand{\BookSection@url}{}

% Between number and title in the head. A knob because "2.5: Title" is a house
% style, not a fact about the book.
\providecommand{\BookSectionSeparator}{:\space}

% The unlinked form, and the one every degraded path falls back to. An empty
% number (the \lecturetitle case) prints the title alone.
\newcommand{\BookSection@plain}{%
  \ifdefempty{\BookSectionNumber}%
    {\BookSectionTitle}%
    {\BookSectionNumber\BookSectionSeparator\BookSectionTitle}%
}

% What the class prints. Empty until one of the two commands runs, so a master
% that declares neither gets no head at all -- the same degrade-to-nothing rule
% the rest of the package follows.
%
% The \href decision is deferred to HERE, at shipout, rather than taken when the
% coordinate is declared. Hyperlinks.sty loads hyperref from an \AtEndPreamble
% hook, so \href does not yet exist while a preamble is being read; testing at
% use time means \booksection works from the preamble or the body without the
% author having to know that. The URL itself is still assembled by \edef at
% declaration time, because \href will not expand macros in its first argument.
\providecommand{\BookSectionHead}{}
\newcommand{\BookSection@head}{%
  \ifdefempty{\BookSection@url}%
    {\BookSection@plain}%
    {\ifdefined\href
       \expandafter\href\expandafter{\BookSection@url}{\BookSection@plain}%
     \else
       % No hyperref (an Assessment master): the coordinate still reads right.
       \BookSection@plain
     \fi}%
}

% Builds the URL, if there is one to build, from what has already been stored.
% It reads the \BookSection* macros rather than taking arguments, because the
% options arrive as macros and the TITLE must never be expanded -- \let-ing the
% pieces into place and resolving here keeps every \xdef away from it.
\newcommand{\BookSection@resolve}{%
  \gdef\BookSection@url{}%
  % A page is the author saying "link this". Only then is a missing base an
  % error worth reporting -- a lecture that gives none simply wants no link,
  % which is the ordinary case for a course with no online text.
  \ifdefempty{\BookSectionSlug}{}{%
    \TextRef@resolvebase{\BookSectionText}%
    \ifdefempty{\TextRef@base}%
      {\TextRef@basemiss{\BookSectionText}}%
      {\xdef\BookSection@url{\TextRef@base\BookSectionSlug}}%
  }%
  \global\let\BookSectionHead\BookSection@head
}

\define@key{booksection}{text}{\def\BookSection@optext{#1}}
\define@key{booksection}{link}{\def\BookSection@optlink{#1}}

\NewDocumentCommand{\booksection}{O{} m m}{%
  \let\BookSection@optext\TextRef@defaulttext
  \def\BookSection@optlink{}%
  \setkeys{booksection}{#1}%
  \gdef\BookSectionNumber{#2}%
  \gdef\BookSectionTitle{#3}%
  \global\let\BookSectionText\BookSection@optext
  \global\let\BookSectionSlug\BookSection@optlink
  \BookSection@resolve
}

\newcommand{\lecturetitle}[1]{%
  \gdef\BookSectionText{}%
  \gdef\BookSectionNumber{}%
  \gdef\BookSectionTitle{#1}%
  \gdef\BookSectionSlug{}%
  \BookSection@resolve
}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% \textbookref
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
% \textbookref{5.2}{Table 5.2.3}       -> "Table 5.2.3", linked to section 5.2
% \textbookref*{5.2.3}{Table}          -> "Table 5.2.3", linked to section 5.2
% \textbookref[algtrig]{3.2}{\S3.2}    -> linked into ANOTHER book entirely
%
% The starred form is the typing shortcut for the overwhelmingly common shape
% "Word Number": it appends the key you already typed (unbreakable ~, since a
% label must never split from its number). Use the plain form whenever the
% printed text is not simply label-then-key ("the start of \S5.2", "the
% definition of flux"). Star first, then the book: \textbookref*[algtrig]{...}.
%
% This is the reference \booksection does NOT cover: a pointer to some OTHER
% section, typically a prerequisite ("if limit laws feel distant, re-read 1.3").
% Those still resolve through the \DeclareSection manifest, which therefore only
% ever needs the handful of sections a course actually cross-references.
%
% NAMING A BOOK resolves the key in that book's namespace and hangs the slug off
% THAT book's base, which is what lets a course point at a text it does not
% teach from -- the prerequisite algebra book behind a calculus course. Omitted,
% everything behaves exactly as it always has: the empty namespace, resolved
% against the document's own default text. The two do not mix, and the reason is
% under \DeclareSection.
\NewDocumentCommand{\textbookref}{s O{} m m}{%
  \IfBooleanTF{#1}{\def\TextRef@text{#4~#3}}{\def\TextRef@text{#4}}%
  % Whose base: the book named, or -- named none -- the document's own.
  \ifstrempty{#2}%
    {\let\TextRef@refbook\TextRef@defaulttext}%
    {\def\TextRef@refbook{#2}}%
  \TextRef@resolve{#2}{#3}%
  \ifdefempty{\TextRef@slug}%
    {\TextRef@miss{#2}{#3}\TextRef@text}%
    {\TextRef@resolvebase{\TextRef@refbook}%
     \ifdefempty{\TextRef@base}%
       % The section resolved but the text it hangs off did not, so there is no
       % URL to build. Report it: a manifest full of good sections and no base
       % would otherwise degrade the whole document in silence.
       {\TextRef@basemiss{\TextRef@refbook}\TextRef@text}%
       {\ifdefined\href
          % \href does catcode surgery on its first argument and will NOT expand
          % macros there, so the URL is assembled by \edef first and handed over
          % pre-tokenised. Consequence: a slug must be plain text -- write \% and
          % \# in the manifest if a URL ever needs them.
          \edef\TextRef@url{\TextRef@base\TextRef@slug}%
          \expandafter\href\expandafter{\TextRef@url}{\TextRef@text}%
        \else
          % No hyperref (an Assessment master): the text is still right.
          \TextRef@text
        \fi}}%
}
